--- a/docs/ecommerce/payments.mdx
+++ b/docs/ecommerce/payments.mdx
@@ -79,6 +79,23 @@ If a transaction is already `succeeded` and has exactly one matching bidirection
 
 Database adapters with transaction support roll back the complete settlement on failure. Without database transactions, the atomic status claim still prevents concurrent winners, but a process crash can leave a transaction in `processing`. Payload does not reset that state automatically because it cannot safely infer whether order, cart, or inventory side effects completed. Monitor and manually reconcile stale `processing` transactions. A future inventory-reservation model can provide stronger recovery semantics.
 
+### Settling from the server
+
+A customer who pays and closes the tab before the confirm-order request runs is charged, and no order exists. An adapter whose provider reports payments to the server, through a webhook or a reconciliation job, settles the transaction with `settleTransaction`. It runs the same atomic claim and side effects as confirm-order, so a duplicate notification or a concurrent confirm-order returns the existing order and writes nothing.
+
+```ts
+import { settleTransaction } from '@payloadcms/plugin-ecommerce'
+
+// After the provider confirms the amount, currency and status of the payment:
+const order = await settleTransaction({
+  orderData: { amount, currency, customerEmail, items, status: 'processing' },
+  req,
+  transactionID,
+})
+```
+
+`settleTransaction` takes the cart from the transaction's `cart` field, and the collection slugs from the plugin's config, which `getEcommerceCollectionSlugs(payload.config)` also returns. It throws when the transaction cannot be settled, for example because its status is `failed` or `expired`. When another request is still settling the transaction after a short wait, it throws `SettlementInProgressError`, and confirm-order answers `409` with `{ status: 'processing' }`. Confirm again to get the order.
+
 ### Stripe Webhooks
 
 You can also add your own webhooks to handle [events from Stripe](https://docs.stripe.com/api/events). This is optional and the plugin internally does not use webhooks for any core functionality. It receives the following arguments:
